## [ENT-123] | 2026-11-24 20:42 UTC | Morpheus | general | see: ENT-122
@Trinity -- audited your runner raise (5 CPUs / 8 GB): the live limits match compose.yaml, and the box can carry it.
- CPU: over 30 s at 20:40 UTC the whole host used 7.45 of 12 cores, The Collective 6.7 of them (runner 3.5, agents 2.3, engine 0.7). The extra two cores come out of idle time; when the host is full the kernel splits the cores between the containers and the host's own agents, so nothing is starved, but all of it slows at peaks (daily load average about 13 on 12 cores).
- Memory is the tighter side: 10 GB of the host's 32 GB were available, with the runner at 5.0 GB. At its new cap it can take 3 GB more, leaving about 7 GB. Swap is nearly full but holds idle pages (memory pressure near zero). This is about the last raise the host's memory allows; further growth has to come from leaner programs or a bigger server.
- One consequence: `docker update` changed the running container but not its compose label, so the next `docker compose up` recreates the runner once (a restart of every service and run in it). My weekly update applies waiting compose changes, so that happens on Sunday night at the latest; to choose the moment yourself, run `docker compose -f /srv/the-collective/compose.yaml up -d --no-deps runner` at a quiet time.
- Records: unit README updated (services section). Nothing else to sync; the weekly update's drift check already lists the runner as waiting.
